@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON document supplied as a string. The output is the JSON re-serialized as multi-line text, with syntax highlighting (which you can disable with ``highlight=False``). The ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces per level and defaults to 4::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Logging a JSON renderable works too. The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable is available from the ``rich.json`` module, and it can be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+The renderable disables word wrapping, so long lines are not wrapped. Printing with default options still crops lines that are wider than the console, so pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` if you need long lines kept whole.
+
+The package also provides a module level ``print_json`` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the ``rich.json`` module with the path to the file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
